+{
+  "schema_version": "review-ledger/v2",
+  "date": "2026-08-03",
+  "session_slug": "equipment-art-resolver-and-ratchet",
+  "task_title": "Themed equipment art resolver generalization + shrink-only equipment art coverage ratchet",
+  "estimated_apples": 3,
+  "stages": {
+    "plan_review": {
+      "completed": true,
+      "reviewer_model": "gpt-5.6-sol",
+      "concerns_count": 4,
+      "resolved_count": 4,
+      "plan_divergence": "minor",
+      "notes": "Blocking (1): variant identity diverged across panels - EquipmentUI.createGeneratedItemIcon fell back to createItemIcon(artKey), seeding the variant pick with the runtime artKey, while InventoryUI and generated-equipment-icon.ts seed with baseId, so one item could render two different variants in the same run. FIXED: createGeneratedItemIcon now takes baseId and resolves/seeds by it at both call sites. Non-blocking (2): the stated precedence was not actually encoded - tier was compared before concept provenance, so a BARE themed entry would outrank the item's own VERSIONED art. FIXED: added provenance ranks (RANK_OWN_REAL < RANK_THEMED_REAL < RANK_PLACEHOLDER) applied before tier, with tier demoted to a tie-break inside a rank; two regression tests added (own-versioned beats themed-bare; themed-real beats own-placeholder). Non-blocking (3): the ratchet could be laundered by shrinking the enumerated ID space - stale baseline ids counted as success, and generated-only equippables were skipped on an unverified assumption that the Floor 2 pool covered them. FIXED: staleBaselineIds now fail the check until acknowledged with --update, and collectRows throws if any catalog-less equippable is absent from FLOOR2_REWARD_POOL_STABLE_IDS. Non-blocking (4): theme overlap behaviour was undefined - two themes claiming one slug resolve by registry order. NOT a bug (one item renders one sprite and the order is deterministic) but was undocumented; the overlap policy is now stated explicitly on EQUIPMENT_THEME_SETS: array order is priority, promote a wave by moving its row up. Probes answered: the fixed RESOLUTION_SEED in the coverage check is sound because tier/rank selection precedes seeded variant choice, so a player seed can never turn real into placeholder. Alternatives enumerated and argued against: emitting runtime-key aliases at manifest-shard build time (reintroduces alias collisions, duplicates texture identity, complicates seeded variant selection); an immediate hard coverage gate (would block all equipment work while 59 gaps remain - revisit once the baseline reaches zero); storing theme membership in manifest metadata (asset metadata cannot represent MISSING art and cannot map gameplay stable IDs/runtime keys). Plan divergence judged minor: the plan said 38 gaps, the baseline is 59 because the plan counted only the Floor 2 art-definition space and missed 25 legacy catalog equippables - corrected enumeration, not a design change."
+    },
+    "code_review": {
+      "clean": true,
+      "rounds": [
+        {
+          "round": 1,
+          "models": ["claude-opus-4.6"],
+          "concerns_count": 1,
+          "resolved_count": 1,
+          "clean": true,
+          "notes": "One Medium finding on the complete working-tree state: compareCandidates' docstring still claimed a total order 'within a tier' after the caller switched from filtering by best tier to filtering by best provenance rank, so the now load-bearing tier comparison read as removable dead code. FIXED: the docstring now states the rank invariant explicitly and lists tier as the first tiebreak. The reviewer independently re-derived and confirmed the three fixes made in response to the plan review (provenance rank before tier; EquipmentUI seeding by baseId rather than artKey; stale-baseline-id failure plus the generated-only pool cross-check), verified that deleting resolveBasicLeatherAliasEntry leaves no remaining consumer of runtimeKey-as-texture (EquipmentUI and generated-equipment-icon.ts both fall through to resolveItemSprite), and verified the package-lock.json change is resolved-URL-only with zero integrity-hash changes. All other categories clean: data flow and determinism, API/contracts and cross-layer integration, security, runtime wiring and resource ownership, regression coverage, policy compliance."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
+    "independent_grade": {
